      "problem": "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡y}{{x}^{3}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the logarithm is positive, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}y>0 \\\\ x\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y>0,\\,x\\ne 0\\}.\n$$",
